t}$ | $2k>0$ | $0$ | $-2k<0$ |\n| $v$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nWe conclude that the velocity is maximized when $x=4\\,m.$\n\nNext, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our initial equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=k(8-2x).\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int k(8-2x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=k(8x-{x}^{2})+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=\\sqrt{6}\\,m/s$ when $x=0\\,m,$ we can find the value of the constant $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =k(8x-{x}^{2})+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{6})^{2} & =k(8(0)-(0)^{2})+C \\\\ C & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=k(8x-{x}^{2})+3.\n$$\n\nAlso, since $v=2\\sqrt{5}\\,m/s$ when $x=1\\,m,$ we can find the value of the positive constant $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =k(8x-{x}^{2})+3 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(2\\sqrt{5})^{2} & =k(8(1)-{(1)}^{2})+3 \\\\ 10 & =7k+3 \\\\ 7k & =7 \\\\ k & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =1(8x-{x}^{2})+3 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =16x-2{x}^{2}+6 \\\\ v & =\\pm \\sqrt{-2{x}^{2}+16x+6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can disregard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=\\sqrt{-2{x}^{2}+16x+6}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the maximum velocity:\n\n$$\n{v}_{max}=v(4)=\\sqrt{-2(4)^{2}+16(4)+6}=\\sqrt{38}\\,m/s\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365310 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186365311 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365310 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186365312 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365310 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186365313 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365310 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref