ion of increasing $x$ where $x\\ge 0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin is $x\\,m,$ the velocity is $v\\,m/s,$ and the acceleration is $k(6-x)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ where $k$ is a positive constant. If $P$ has velocity $v=2\\,m/s$ at the origin and has velocity $v=2\\sqrt{11}\\,m/s$ when its displacement is $2\\,m,$ find the maximum velocity of the particle." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365316 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3fbdf53c-2cfd-519b-93e8-373440a987e3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365316 #edb/ref 1074 "![](images/a8/a8ff5e02237554d0f7107168f287adbe1834c4dd7ad965163531f5a23412a4fc.png)\n\nThe extrema of the velocity occur at the critical points of $v.$ Since the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $k(6-x)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=k(6-x).\n$$\n\nTo find the moments where $v$ is a maximum, we need to solve $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=0$ and test each solution using the first derivative test.\n\nSolving $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k(6-x) & =0 \\\\ 6-x & =0 \\\\ x & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the extremum of the velocity function occurs when $x=6\\,m.$\n\nWe now test the extreme value using the first derivative test. We summarize the necessary information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(0,6)$ | $6$ | $(6,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $5$ |  | $7$ |\n| $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$ | $k>0$ | $0$ | $-k<0$ |\n| $v$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nWe conclude that the velocity is maximized when $x=6\\,m.$\n\nNext, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our initial equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=k(6-x).\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int k(6-x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=k(6x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2})+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=2\\,m/s$ when $x=0\\,m,$ we can find the value of the constant $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =k(6x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2})+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(2)^{2} & =k(6(0)-\\frac{1}{2}(0)^{2})+C \\\\ C & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=k(6x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2})+2.\n$$\n\nAlso, since $v=2\\sqrt{11}\\,m/s$ when $x=2\\,m,$ we can find the value of the positive constant $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =k(6x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2})+2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(2\\sqrt{11})^{2} & =k(6(2)-\\frac{1}{2}{(2)}^{2})+2 \\\\ 22 & =10k+2 \\\\ 10k & =20 \\\\ k & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =2(6x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2})+2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =12x-{x}^{2}+2 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =24x-2{x}^{2}+4 \\\\ v & =\\pm \\sqrt{-2{x}^{2}+24x+4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can disregard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=\\sqrt{-2{x}^{2}+24x+4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the maximum velocity:\n\n$$\n{v}_{max}=v(6)=\\sqrt{-2(6)^{2}+24(6)+4}=2\\sqrt{19}\\,m/s\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365317 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186365318 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365317 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186365319 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365317 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186365320 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365317 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186365321 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365317 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186365322 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365317 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365317 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365317 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186365320 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365317 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "4aa0d68b-3aee-5717-aebf-0211366a9b34" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365318 #edb/